{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Oakland Athletics were a team defined by contradictions — a home run machine that couldn't stop the bleeding on the mound, a roster full of individual highlights set against a backdrop of organizational transition. They finished 75-87, missing the playoffs for the fourth straight year, but the season had enough moments of genuine drama to suggest something was quietly being built in the East Bay.\n\nThe A's stumbled out of the gate in April despite a spectacular opening weekend when Ryan Dull served up a three-run blast to Danny Espinosa that ended in heartbreak, a sign of the bullpen troubles to come. Santiago Casilla and Ryan Madson would become recurring villains in the relief corps, coughing up leads to rivals like the Angels in the final days of April. May brought more pain, as Oakland went 12-15 and found itself on the wrong end of late-game collapses, including a brutal sequence against Detroit where Jed Lowrie seemed determined to single-handedly beat his former club.\n\nThe summer months told a story of a team fighting but falling short. July was particularly brutal — a month where Casilla surrendered back-to-back home runs to the Blue Jays in the same game, Blake Treinen got touched up, and even an Rajai Davis solo shot on July 29th came at a moment when Oakland was losing ground. Amid the carnage, though, were glimpses of the future: Matt Chapman, Matt Olson, and Franklin Barreto showed up on the ledger as contributors, for better and worse, and the power numbers were genuinely impressive even as the pitching staff leaked runs at an alarming rate.\n\nSeptember rescued the season's reputation. Oakland went 16-12 in the final full month, their best stretch of baseball all year. Grand slams from Joey Wendle and Jed Lowrie punctuated a genuine late surge, and even the bullpen showed flickers of reliability. It was too little, too late for playoff contention, but the September run offered a tantalizing preview of a younger, hungrier A's team that would make noise in seasons to come.",
